Instruction files like CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md now obey --deny-dir / --allow-dir based on where they actually point. Before this, a CLAUDE.md symlinked into a denied directory went into the system prompt and was sent to the provider, even though read_file refused the same path. Committed as cf8ad734, Part of #1002. Build, fmt, clippy and the full test suite (6319 passed, 0 failed) are all green.

Before and after. I set up a project where CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md are symlinks into a secret/ directory holding a token each, then counted how often each token appeared in --print-system-prompt:

| Run | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| --deny-dir secret, CLAUDE.md token | 1 | 0 |
| --deny-dir secret, AGENTS.md token | 1 | 0 |
| --allow-dir proj, CLAUDE.md token | 1 | 0 |
| --allow-dir proj --allow-dir secret (target is allowed, so it should load) | — | 1 |
| No flags, ordinary CLAUDE.md | loads | loads, no warning, identical output |

What changed:
• **Loader (src/context.rs):** each instruction file that exists is checked before it is read, using the same check the file tools use since #996. I didn't write a second symlink resolver.
• **Refusal warning:** a refused file is skipped with a yellow warning on stderr that names it. It isn't dimmed or hidden in quiet mode, because it's a security fact. Paths in the message are sanitized, and under --screen-reader it starts with plain warning: and contains no symbols.
• **`src/cli.rs`:** the directory restrictions are now built before the instruction files load. That step only reads the command-line arguments and the raw config, so moving it earlier changes nothing else.
• **`src/commands_spawn.rs`:** both spawn paths pass the parent session's restrictions, because the instruction files are read from the parent's directory.
• Tests: new file src/context_fence_tests.rs (unix only, temp directories only) covers the denied link, a link outside the allowed set, the allowed-target case, unchanged output with no restrictions, and the warning text. When I disabled the new check, exactly the two refusal tests failed by name; after restoring it they passed.
• **Existing tests updated:** three tests that pinned the old call text broke. I updated them to the new call text rather than deleting them, and they now also confirm the restrictions are passed at each call site.
• **Docs:** a dated note on the context.rs entry in ARCHITECTURE.md, and one paragraph in docs/src/configuration/permissions.md.

One behaviour change: if --allow-dir doesn't include the current directory, an ordinary CLAUDE.md there is now refused too. That matches what read_file already does with the same file.

Not covered yet (still open under #1002): the goal file, memory.json, skills, the repo map, and the /context file list are still read without these checks. The /context list is only shown to the user, never sent to the provider.
